In C, the best way to return an error is by using the `errno` variable and setting it appropriately. Here's a simple example:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <errno.h>
#include <string.h>

int divide(int numerator, int denominator) {
    if (denominator == 0) {
        errno = EINVAL; // Set errno to EINVAL (Invalid argument)
        return -1; // Return an error code
    }
    return numerator / denominator;
}

int main() {
    int result = divide(10, 0);
    if (result == -1) {
        printf("Error: %s\n", strerror(errno)); // Print the error message
    }
    return 0;
}
```

In this example, if the denominator is zero, we set `errno` to `EINVAL` and return -1 to indicate an error. In the `main` function, we check if the result is -1 and print the corresponding error message using `strerror(errno)`. This way, you can handle errors gracefully in your C programs.

